---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Best Gifts for Cigar Smokers | StickScore Guides"
  description="What actually makes a good cigar gift by price tier — and what to avoid buying someone who already has a setup."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">Best Gifts for Cigar Smokers</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      The best cigar gifts are things people rarely buy for themselves — a nicer cutter than their
      daily one, a good ashtray, a journal — rather than duplicating what they probably already
      own. A humidor is the one major exception: it's a genuinely welcome gift precisely because
      most smokers put off upgrading their own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Under $30: small, personal upgrades</h2>
    <p>
      A quality punch or single cutter, a pocket lighter, or a cigar journal all land well at this
      price — small enough to not feel like a major purchase decision on your behalf, but nice
      enough that most people won't have bought it for themselves. See
      <a href="/accessories/cutters/">cutters</a> and
      <a href="/accessories/journals-stands/">journals</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">$30-100: a step up from what they already own</h2>
    <p>
      A nicer ashtray, a premium double-guillotine or V-cut, or a soft-flame/torch lighter from a
      brand with a real warranty all fit here. This is also the sweet spot for a travel case if you
      know the recipient travels with cigars. See
      <a href="/accessories/ashtrays/">ashtrays</a>,
      <a href="/accessories/torch-lighters/">torch lighters</a>, and
      <a href="/accessories/travel-cases/">travel cases</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">$100+: the big one — a humidor</h2>
    <p>
      A humidor is the gift most cigar smokers delay buying for themselves, which makes it land
      unusually well as a gift. Check what they already own before buying, though — see
      <a href="/guides/best-humidors-under-100/">humidors under $100</a> and
      <a href="/guides/best-humidors-under-200/">under $200</a> for picks by budget, and
      <a href="/guides/desktop-vs-cabinet-vs-travel-humidors/">desktop vs cabinet vs travel</a>
      if you're not sure which type fits their collection size.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">What to avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Skip humidification refills and hygrometers unless you know exactly what system they run —
      these are consumable, personal-preference items, not good surprise gifts. And avoid gifting
      actual cigars unless you know their specific taste well; a gift card to a reputable retailer
      is the safer move there.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
